    <!-- Hilfe -->
    <string name="help_repeat_title">Repeating alarms</string>
    <string name="help_repeat_text">Tap the repeat line of an alarm to choose a rule:\n• Once – on a date or the next time the time comes round\n• Every n days – e.g. every 3rd day\n• Every n weeks on chosen weekdays – e.g. every other week on Monday and Thursday\n• Monthly on a day – e.g. on the 15th or on the last day\n• Monthly on a weekday – e.g. the first and third Thursday or the last Friday\n• Yearly\nEvery rule can have an end date. The preview shows the next occurrences.\nThe weekday circles on the card work like in the Android clock.</string>
    <string name="help_timetable_title">Alternating weeks and timetables</string>
    <string name="help_timetable_text">For a rhythm that changes from week to week – A/B weeks at school, early and late shifts, a uni timetable – create one alarm per pattern with “Every 2 weeks”, e.g. 6:00 on Mon–Wed and 8:30 on Thu–Fri.\nThe start date decides which week counts: the interval is counted from the week of the start date. For the other week, give a second alarm a start date one week later.\nLonger cycles work too, e.g. every 3 or 4 weeks.</string>
    <string name="help_pause_title">Pausing</string>
    <string name="help_pause_text">Repeating alarms can be paused – for holidays, for example. Choose days, weeks or months starting today, a number of occurrences to skip, or a last date. The dialog lists the occurrences that are skipped. After the pause the alarm continues by itself.</string>
    <string name="help_ringing_title">Snooze, volume and sound</string>
    <string name="help_ringing_text">Snooze length and “Gradually louder” can be set per alarm or as a default in the settings. A ringing alarm stops by itself after the time set in the settings and then shows a “Missed alarm” notice.\nYou can use the device’s alarm sounds and ringtones or your own audio file. Your own file is copied into the app so that it still plays after a restart.</string>
    <string name="help_reliability_title">Reliability</string>
    <string name="help_reliability_text">Alarms use Android’s alarm clock function and ring in Doze, battery saver and Do Not Disturb (if alarms are allowed there). After a restart they work even before the first unlock.\nThe app checks permissions, alarm volume and Do Not Disturb and shows warnings in the list and the settings.\nImportant: “Force stop” in the app info deletes all scheduled alarms until you open the app again.\nIf the normal sound cannot start, an emergency alarm with a notification rings instead. If an alarm could not ring (device off, clock changed), you get a “Missed alarm” notice.\nTip: try the test alarm in the settings once with the screen locked.</string>
    <string name="help_flip_title">Turn over to snooze</string>
    <string name="help_flip_text">While an alarm is ringing, place the phone face down to snooze it. After the snooze time it rings again. If the phone is still lying face down, pick it up and put it down again – or tap “Snooze”. The motion sensor only runs while an alarm is ringing. You can turn this off in the settings.</string>
    <string name="help_privacy_title">Privacy</string>
    <string name="help_privacy_text">The app has no internet permission. All alarms stay on your device.</string>
